# Gap fixture archive — Swift backend array-boundary conversion gap (switch/try paths)

Self-contained archive of the Haxe→Swift counterexample fixture `gap.Gap`
(`Gap.hx`), its exact generation driver, its observed generated output, and the
`swiftc -typecheck` evidence. Origin: `/home/losses/Development/tq-workspace/dc-warn/out/switch-try-gap-verify/`
(scratch tree of an adversarial verification run, 2026-09-30; its report is copied
here verbatim as `REPORT.md`). Archived 2026-09-30 from that tree, then reproduced
from this archive (see "Reproduction").

## The defect, in one paragraph

The Swift backend converts a mutable `TiqianArray<Element>` into a
`ReadOnlyArray<Element>` (two unrelated `final` classes; no implicit conversion)
through a small funnel — `SwiftArrayBoundary.prepare`/`render`, entered from
`SwiftExpr.lowerArrayBoundary` and from `SwiftDecl`'s static-field path. That
conversion is applied on some expression paths (ternary initializers, statement-
position switch/try assignments, static fields) but **not** on the switch/try
*value* paths: switch or try used as an initializer, in return position, as a call
argument, or on the right-hand side of an assignment. For those seven shapes the
emitted Swift puts a raw `TiqianArray<Int32>` where `ReadOnlyArray<Int32>` is
required, and `swiftc -typecheck` fails with **12 type errors** (plus 2
unused-result warnings marking a related swallowed-`return` symptom). The precise
mechanism: in `stmtLines` the switch/try dispatch cases match *before* the general
case that computes the boundary and calls `lowerArrayBoundary`, and the dedicated
emitters (`switchExpression`, `tryBindingLines`, `tryReturnLines`, `switchAssign`,
`switchReturn` via expression arms) never consult the destination type.

